1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,200
Anyway so I was going to talk about the migration but I should probably back up.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:11,000
Because there's context, and the context is sort of the whole thing really.

3
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:16,800
We had 47 services. Or 51, depending on whether you count the cron boxes.

4
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:22,600
And nobody, and I do mean nobody, had a complete picture of it in their head.

5
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:28,400
Which, you know, that's normal, that's just what happens after four years.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,200
Um. So the plan, originally, was to do it in three phases over two quarters.

7
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:40,000
That did not happen. Obviously. I don't think it's ever happened anywhere.

8
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:45,800
And it wasn't even the technical stuff that got us, which is the annoying part.

9
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:51,600
It was that two teams had different definitions of the word "customer".

10
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:57,400
Not slightly different. Structurally different. One included trial accounts.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,200
Anyway that came out in week six, which was, you can imagine, a fun week.

12
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:09,000
Where was I. Right, the phases. So phase one was supposed to be read-only.

13
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:14,800
Except read-only isn't really a thing when you have write-through caching.

14
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:20,600
I keep meaning to write this up properly and I keep not doing it.

15
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:26,400
Someone asked me at a conference and I gave a much better answer than this.

16
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,200
The honest summary is we spent nine months on something we scoped at four.

17
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:38,000
And I still think it was worth doing, which people find annoying when I say it.

18
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:43,800
Because the alternative was another four years of nobody understanding it.

19
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:49,600
Um, what else. Oh, the rollback. We had a rollback plan and it was fiction.

20
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:55,400
Everybody's rollback plan is fiction. If you've never tested it, it's a story.

21
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,200
We found that out at 2am on a Thursday, which is when you always find that out.

22
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:07,000
Anyway. I don't really have a conclusion. It went fine in the end, mostly.

23
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:12,800
Three people left, which I don't think was the migration, but who knows.

24
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:18,600
So yeah. Test your rollback. That's probably the one actionable thing here.
